# Typography

Set all text in Arial, using 2 weights: regular and bold. Take every size from the type scale on this page, so a room name, a reading and a heading look the same on every dashboard.

Arial, or the very similar Helvetica, is already on most computers, iPhones and iPads. Android devices use their own sans-serif font instead. So Home Assistant never has to download a font before it shows the dashboard.

## Typeface

Use this font stack in a custom card or a theme:

```css
font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
```

If Arial is missing, the device uses Helvetica, then its own sans-serif font. Do not add another typeface to the stack, and do not download or bundle a font with a card. The civica wordmark is artwork, not a font. Do not use it to set text.

The civica theme sets the Home Assistant font variables for you, including `ha-font-family-body` and `ha-font-family-heading`. Code editors keep a monospace font, `'Courier New', monospace`, because the alignment of code carries meaning.

### Weights

| Weight | Value | Use it for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Regular | 400 | Body text, labels, hints, readings and buttons |
| Bold | 700 | Headings, and a few words that need emphasis |

Do not use a light or medium weight. Most devices have neither for Arial, so they show regular instead and the difference you designed disappears. The civica theme sets Home Assistant's medium weight, `ha-font-weight-medium`, to 400 for this reason.

