### Native Home Assistant sizes

Native Home Assistant screens are denser than a civica card, so they keep smaller sizes. The civica theme raises each native size by one step, to these values:

| Home Assistant variable | Size | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ha-font-size-l` | 19px | Larger native text |
| `ha-font-size-m` | 16px | Native controls and body text |
| `ha-font-size-s` | 14px | Supporting labels |
| `ha-font-size-xs` | 12px | Compact extra text, such as a secondary detail in a row |

Do not make every native row bigger to match a civica card. That pushes controls off the screen and breaks the native layout. Check native screens at 200% text size instead.

### Set sizes in a card

Use the variable, not a pixel value, so the size follows the font size scale. Each variable is written as a `calc()` that multiplies the base size by `--ha-font-size-scale`.

```css
.reading-label {
  font-size: var(--civica-font-size-small);
  line-height: 1.5;
}
```

Heading cards already use the card heading size. The civica theme sets `ha-heading-card-title-font-size` to the card heading variable, with weight 700 and line height 1.25. This keeps a section title as strong as the cards under it. Subtitles keep their native supporting style.

```yaml
type: heading
heading: Living room
heading_style: title
```

## Headings

Use one main heading on a page, and keep heading levels in order. Choose the level for the structure of the page, not for the size you want. If a heading looks too big, use a different size. Do not skip a level.

A large reading, such as “21°C”, is a value, not a heading. Do not mark it up as a heading to make it bigger, and do not make a paragraph look like a heading while leaving it as a paragraph. Screen reader users move around a page by its headings.

## Write readable text

- Use sentence case for headings, card names and buttons. Do not use capitals for a whole label.
- Use bold for a few words, not whole paragraphs.
- Do not use italics for an instruction people need to follow.
- Do not use pale grey text. Use the supporting text colour on [Web and app colours](colour-web.html#functional-colours).
- Let a long room or device name wrap onto 2 lines. Do not cut it off with “…” if people need it to tell 2 devices apart.

